<launch>
  <!--
    exploration.launch — the full FALCON exploration, flown on the SJTU drone.

    A lean port of sjtu_project's gazebo_exploration.launch. What was dropped
    is not lost: the noise-injection adapter, recorder, BEV publisher and
    completion watcher belonged to that stack's experiment harness, and
    cmd_to_vel is replaced by our bspline_follower (which reads the CURVE, not
    the 100 Hz samples — see bspline_follower.launch for why that matters).

    Topology, three nodes plus FALCON's two:

      /simple_drone/odom  (bridged from ROS2, body-frame twist)
              │
        sensor_pose_node ──► /map_ros/pose   (camera pose, mapper pairs by stamp)
              │        └───► /odom_world     (twist rotated to WORLD — FALCON
              │                               reads it unrotated into the next
              │                               plan's initial velocity)
      /simple_drone/front_depth/depth/image_raw (bridged, 32FC1 metres)
              │
        exploration_node  (mapper + frontiers + planner + FSM, unmodified)
              │
        traj_server ──► /planning/pos_cmd    (unused by us, kept for FALCON)
              └───────► /planning/bspline ──► bspline_follower ──► cmd_vel
  -->

  <arg name="map_name" default="small_house"/>
  <!-- Forwarded to the follower; see bspline_follower.launch for what it does
       and why the burden of proof is on turning it on. Declared here because an
       include passes only the args it names, so a knob that is not forwarded is
       silently the follower default however it is set on the command line. -->
  <arg name="unknown_brake" default="false"/>
  <arg name="drone_ns" default="/simple_drone"/>

  <!-- ONE clock owns everything. Every data stamp in this graph is already
       Gazebo sim time (the bridged depth, odom and therefore /map_ros/pose),
       but without this flag ros::Time::now() was WALL time: FALCON stamped
       B-splines on a clock ~12% faster than the physics (hospital rtf ~0.88),
       so the reference ran ahead of the aircraft and the follower burned its
       catch-up margin closing a gap that was pure clock skew. /clock is
       bridged ROS2->ROS1 in config/bridge.yaml (best_effort, matching its
       publisher); nodes idle until the bridge comes up, which the run script
       tolerates. -->
  <param name="/use_sim_time" value="true"/>

  <!-- ── Speeds. Yaw faster than translation so the camera can align at a door
       before the aircraft is committed through it.

       0.15, down from the old stack's 0.25, and the number comes out of the
       doorway rather than out of comfort. What decides whether an aircraft can
       thread a 0.93 m opening is not its speed but its CORRECTION PER METRE
       TRAVELLED: how much cross-track it can take out before it arrives. The
       follower's per-tick ceiling is `max_vel + max_overspeed`, so that ratio
       is max_overspeed / max_vel, and the ceiling itself is bounded by
       clearance (see the speed block below). With the ceiling pinned at
       0.35 m/s the only way to buy correction is to lower the plan:

         config                        plan  ceiling  corr  ratio  stopping
         run 003 (finished the world)  0.25   0.60    0.35   1.40   0.41 m
         ceiling capped, plan kept     0.25   0.35    0.10   0.40   0.18 m
         ceiling capped, plan 0.20     0.20   0.35    0.15   0.75   0.18 m
         this                          0.15   0.35    0.20   1.33   0.18 m

       The middle two both stalled at hospital doorways with zero contacts, at
       297 m3 and 211 m3, having simply run out of authority to centre. This
       one restores run 003's ratio while keeping its stopping distance inside
       the 0.19 m the planner reserves. The cost is wall-clock. -->
  <arg name="max_vel"     default="0.15"/>
  <arg name="max_acc"     default="0.25"/>
  <arg name="max_yaw_vel" default="0.6"/>
  <arg name="max_yaw_acc" default="0.5"/>

  <!-- ── Safety. READ THIS BEFORE TUNING EITHER NUMBER.
       `obstacles_inflation` DOES NOTHING. The string "inflation" does not occur
       anywhere in FALCON's source — `/voxel_mapping/obstacles_inflation` is
       read by no node, so every value it has ever been given, and every
       conclusion drawn from changing it (including the aisle-sealing notes in
       config/warehouse.yaml), is about a parameter that was never consumed. It
       is kept only so the old command lines do not break, and so the next
       person finds this note instead of re-deriving it.

       `safe_distance` is the ONLY clearance the planner respects. It is used
       in BsplineOptimizer::calcDistanceCost against the 3D ESDF:

           getDistanceAndGradient(q[i], dist, dist_grad);
           if (dist < safe_distance_) cost += pow(dist - safe_distance_, 2);

       Three consequences. It is ISOTROPIC — the same margin over and under an
       obstacle as beside it, which is the only vertical clearance in the
       stack. It is SOFT — a penalty weighed against smoothness and dynamic
       feasibility, so the optimiser will spend it to keep a curve flyable. And
       at the old 0.15 m it asked for less margin than the aircraft's own
       0.25 m radius, while the follower's map gate vetoes anything inside
       0.28 m — so the planner's best legal curve sat inside the gate's trip
       line and the two fought each other.
       0.35 m: above the gate's bubble, and a 1.4 m warehouse aisle still
       leaves 0.35 m of slack on each side. -->
  <arg name="obstacles_inflation" default="0.25"/>
  <arg name="safe_distance"       default="0.55"/>

  <!-- ── Frontier viewpoints: clearances lowered (0.35 to 0.25, 0.60 to 0.40) so
       FALCON places a viewpoint in a tighter space and observes a frontier
       tucked behind a doorway, instead of declaring it un-viewable and leaving
       that room unmapped. rmin stays 1.0 (above the 0.95 m near clip) so a
       viewpoint can still actually see what it is placed to observe. -->
  <arg name="frontier_min_clearance"     default="0.25"/>
  <arg name="frontier_min_occ_clearance" default="0.40"/>
  <arg name="frontier_candidate_rmin"    default="1.0"/>
  <arg name="frontier_candidate_rmax"    default="2.5"/>

  <!-- ── FALCON's own configuration, exactly as its exploration.launch loads
       it. The map yaml is OURS, mounted over the image's config/map/ dir by
       run_falcon_sjtu.sh. -->
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find exploration_manager)/config/robot/uav_model_simulator.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find exploration_manager)/config/map/$(arg map_name).yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find voxel_mapping)/config/voxel_mapping.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find pathfinding)/config/astar.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find fast_planner)/config/fast_planner.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find exploration_preprocessing)/config/frontier_finder.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find exploration_preprocessing)/config/hgrid.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find exploration_utils)/config/perception_utils.yaml"/>
  <rosparam command="load" file="$(find exploration_manager)/config/exploration_manager.yaml"/>

  <!-- ── Overrides. NO sensing_parameters here: the corrected camera model
       (600x600, fx=fy=390.64 — the previous stack's 640x480/320 corrupted the
       map, see the package README) is owned by bspline_follower.launch, ONE
       source of truth, included below. -->
  <param name="/transformer/pose_topic_type"     value="pose"/>
  <param name="/transformer/timestamp_tolerance" value="0.05"/>

  <param name="/uav_model/dynamics_parameters/max_linear_velocity"     value="$(arg max_vel)"/>
  <param name="/uav_model/dynamics_parameters/max_linear_acceleration" value="$(arg max_acc)"/>
  <param name="/uav_model/dynamics_parameters/max_yaw_velocity"        value="$(arg max_yaw_vel)"/>
  <param name="/uav_model/dynamics_parameters/max_yaw_acceleration"    value="$(arg max_yaw_acc)"/>

  <param name="/voxel_mapping/obstacles_inflation" value="$(arg obstacles_inflation)"/>
  <param name="/bspline_opt/safe_distance"         value="$(arg safe_distance)"/>

  <!-- ── Coverage-tour TSP size vs LKH stability. FALCON's coverage tour is a
       TSP over hgrid cell centres, solved in-process by LKH, which segfaults
       intermittently and more often as the tour grows (FindTour -> Best5OptMove
       -> Flip_SL). Coarser cells mean far fewer of them — a 40x40 m box drops
       from ~64 five-metre cells to ~16 ten-metre ones — which shrinks the TSP
       and the crash rate while keeping the whole hospital in scope. The tour is
       slightly less optimal; that is the trade for surviving. -->
  <!-- 10.0, and it stays there. It was briefly raised to 14.0 on the theory
       that the node's aborts were LKH crashes scaling with the tour size. That
       theory is wrong: the measured stack trace is an Eigen assertion in the
       RViz drawBspline path, nothing to do with the TSP (see the crash section
       in the README), and 10.0 is what the one run that mapped the whole
       hospital used. Coarser cells make the coverage tour worse for no gain. -->
  <arg name="hgrid_cell_size_max" default="10.0"/>
  <param name="/hgrid/cell_size_max" value="$(arg hgrid_cell_size_max)"/>

  <!-- ── Do not choose a viewpoint the aircraft is already standing on ────
       getTopViewpointsInfo takes the first viewpoint at least this far from the
       current pose. At 0.5 m FALCON regularly picks one essentially on top of
       the aircraft, and the trajectory it then fits is a near-zero-length
       curve. That is the direct precursor of the crash that dominates this
       deployment: evaluating such a curve outside its own knot span trips an
       Eigen assertion inside PlanningVisualization::drawBspline and aborts the
       node, which wipes the map (see "A planner respawn wipes the map" in the
       README for the measured stack trace and what it costs).

       1.2 m is above the 0.6 m the dead-end guard already calls "at target",
       so it does not shadow a viewpoint the aircraft could usefully fly to,
       and it is below candidate_rmin 1.0 plus the 0.5 m grid the viewpoints
       are sampled on, so it does not empty the candidate set. -->
  <arg name="frontier_min_candidate_dist" default="1.2"/>
  <param name="/frontier_finder/min_candidate_dist" value="$(arg frontier_min_candidate_dist)"/>

  <param name="/frontier_finder/min_candidate_clearance"          value="$(arg frontier_min_clearance)"/>
  <param name="/frontier_finder/min_candidate_occupied_clearance" value="$(arg frontier_min_occ_clearance)"/>
  <param name="/frontier_finder/candidate_rmin"                   value="$(arg frontier_candidate_rmin)"/>
  <param name="/frontier_finder/candidate_rmax"                   value="$(arg frontier_candidate_rmax)"/>

  <!-- Dead-end guard (patched into exploration_manager + frontier_finder): when
       the aircraft sits pinned short of a chosen viewpoint for 20 s, that
       viewpoint is handed to the frontier finder, which retires every cluster
       whose average is within this radius to dormant so the coverage tour stops
       re-selecting a route the map thinks is clear but physics blocks.

       This knob traded completeness against termination, and it was set at 3.5
       for termination: parts of both worlds are genuinely unobservable and the
       mission cannot FINISH until those frontiers retire (the hospital's two
       north corner rooms are filled by staircases climbing z 0 to 3.0, both its
       elevators are modelled as solid door slabs, seven cubicle curtains are
       solid from 0.095 to 2.483 m). 3.5 exceeded candidate_rmax (2.5), so
       retiring a viewpoint also retired the frontier that produced it, which
       stopped run 002 making twenty-three attempts on one north-west pocket.

       That was the wrong half of the trade, and the note here used to say what
       would prove it: lower this if a run ever finishes with a room genuinely
       missing rather than merely unreachable. One did. On 2026-08-14 the guard
       blocked (5.11, -1.21) at t = 53 s, eight seconds into the flight with
       almost nothing observed yet, and at 3.5 m that shadow covered the
       corridor joining the north wing to the south. The aircraft mapped the
       north, ran out of frontiers and declared FINISH at 253.65 m3, with the
       entire southern half of the building never visited. A shadow cast over a
       TRANSIT route is not the same mistake as one cast over a dead-end pocket,
       and permanence made the first one fatal.

       The resolution is not to pick a side of the trade but to put time into
       it, which falcon_blocked_region_ttl.patch does. A shadow now expires, and
       each re-report of the same region DOUBLES the next one (capped at
       2^ttl_max_doubling). An early mistake in a corridor is forgiven after
       90 s; a pocket that really is unobservable is re-reported and retires for
       90, 180, 360, then 720 s, which is the rest of the mission. Termination
       survives, completeness stops depending on the first minute of the flight.

       With expiry doing the work, the radius goes back under candidate_rmax so
       one bad viewpoint no longer retires its whole frontier. -->
  <param name="/frontier_finder/blocked_region_radius" value="2.0"/>
  <!-- ...and that is right exactly ONCE. Measured on 2026-08-15 in both halves
       of the hospital: a shadow is cast at the VIEWPOINT the aircraft could not
       reach, but a frontier is retired only when a shadow falls within the
       radius of the frontier's AVERAGE, and viewpoints are sampled
       candidate_rmin..rmax (1.0-2.5 m) from that average by construction. With
       2.0 under candidate_rmax 2.5 the test can never fire for the frontier
       that produced the blocked viewpoint. The frontier survives, samples
       another viewpoint centimetres away, and the tour offers it forever. Run
       h01 aimed at three viewpoints of one frontier 91/50/28 times and died of no
       growth with the north half of the building never visited; run h02 aimed
       at (-11.57, 10.16) 95 times while the guard reported the aircraft
       confined short of that same point four separate times. The TTL ladder
       cannot help: a shadow that never matches anything is refreshed forever
       at no cost.

       So the radius escalates on the SAME ladder as the TTL, under its own
       cap. Strike 1 stays at 2.0 m and lifts in 90 s, which is what protects a
       transit corridor from one early mistake: the 253.65 m3 failure above.
       From strike 2 the shadow exceeds candidate_rmax and the frontier finally
       goes dormant, which is the termination property the flat 3.5 used to
       buy, now paid for with evidence: two independent 25 s confinements at
       one place, keyed on the coarse 4 m history below.

       Set this at or below blocked_region_radius to disable the widening and
       get the old fixed-width behaviour back. -->
  <param name="/frontier_finder/blocked_region_radius_max" value="3.5"/>
  <param name="/frontier_finder/blocked_region_ttl_s" value="90.0"/>
  <!-- 5, so the ladder is 90, 180, 360, 720, 1440, 2880 s. The cap is what
       decides whether a mission can TERMINATE: at 3 (720 s) the hospital's last
       unreachable frontier came back twice inside one run, and FALCON spent the
       endgame re-attempting it, 1911 "No path to next viewpoint" lines, holding
       at 736.3 m3 until the no-growth watchdog ended a mission that had already
       mapped the building. A place that has independently defeated the aircraft
       five times is not worth offering again, and at 1440 s it is retired for
       the rest of any run this campaign flies, which lets the frontier set
       actually empty and the FSM reach FINISH. -->
  <param name="/frontier_finder/blocked_region_ttl_max_doubling" value="5"/>
  <!-- Strikes are keyed more coarsely than shadows, and on a history that
       never expires. An aircraft defeated twice by the same pile of clutter
       does not fail at the same point twice: measured 2.6 m apart in the
       hospital's north-west corner, which is outside the 2.0 m coalesce
       radius, so both failures read as new, both shadows started at strike 1,
       and the escalation above never engaged at all. The aircraft went back
       into that corner and ground on it for 297 s of a 485 s run, 78 bumper
       reports on one clutter pile. 4.0 m spans an obstacle and the pocket
       around it without reaching the next room. -->
  <param name="/frontier_finder/blocked_region_escalate_radius" value="4.0"/>

  <!-- ── A*: give it time to find the route, and cells to find it through ─
       These override two values loaded from pathfinding/config/astar.yaml, and
       they are the difference between an exploration that reaches the far end
       of a building and one that declares itself finished a third of the way
       in.

       The DEFAULT profile is the search `planTrajToView` runs to route the
       aircraft to its chosen viewpoint, once per replan (~2.5 s). Upstream
       gives it `max_search_time: 0.001`, ONE MILLISECOND, over a 0.5 m
       lattice. Across a 24 x 56 m hospital that budget expires long before the
       frontier is reached, and FALCON reports "No path to next viewpoint",
       eventually retires the cluster as unreachable, and stops. Measured, two
       runs of the same configuration: the one that mapped the whole building
       logged 0 no-path lines, the one that finished at 260.8 m3 having never
       gone south of y = -2.3 logged 58.

       0.05 s is still 5% of one replan cycle and two orders of magnitude more
       search. The COARSE profiles are deliberately left alone: they build the
       tour's cost matrix over many pairs at once and are meant to degenerate
       to a straight-line estimate.

       Resolution 0.5 -> 0.3 for the same reason from the other direction: a
       0.93 m doorway is TWO cells on a 0.5 m lattice, so a single occupied
       cell closes it to the search, and the aircraft is asked to reach rooms
       it can see through a door the planner thinks is a wall. At 0.3 m it is
       three cells. The cost is ~4.6x the cells, which the time budget above
       now covers. -->
  <!-- allocate_num is the A* NODE POOL, and it has to move with the
       resolution or refining the lattice makes the search worse, not better.
       Upstream pairs 100000 nodes with a 0.5 m grid. At 0.3 m the same volume
       needs (0.5/0.3)^3 = 4.6x as many, the pool runs out mid-search, and every
       query returns no-path: measured 2012 "No path to next viewpoint" lines
       in one run against 58 in a healthy one, after which FALCON went silent,
       the follower's respawn-recovery survey fired seven times, and the
       aircraft spent 93% of its ticks turning on the spot until the watchdog
       ended it for not moving. 500000 nodes is about 25 MB. -->
  <!-- RESOLUTION IS BACK AT UPSTREAM'S 0.5, and the round trip is the lesson.
       0.3 was chosen to give a 0.93 m doorway three cells instead of two, on
       the theory that one occupied cell was closing doors to the search. That
       theory was never confirmed and the change did measurable harm: it made
       every query 4.6x more expensive, and after a planner respawn, when the
       map is empty and A* must expand through unknown space with nothing to
       guide it, searches stopped completing at all. 976 no-path lines in one
       run, coverage frozen, against ZERO in the run that mapped the whole
       building on upstream's 0.5 m.
       The time budget stays raised, because one millisecond is indefensible on
       any lattice, and the pool stays large because headroom is free. -->
  <!-- ── the coverage tour's connectivity slice ────────────────────────────
       Under map_dimension 2 the tour reduces every hgrid cell by connected-
       component labelling on ONE horizontal slice, and the height of that slice
       was the literal 1.0 in getCCLCenters2D. It is now a parameter, defaulting
       to 1.0 so an unset config is upstream behaviour.

       1.0 is 0.1 m off the hospital's box floor (0.9) and nowhere near the
       1.15-1.49 m the aircraft flies. It is, however, exactly where this
       building keeps its beds, gurneys, wheelchairs, sinks and carts, which run
       0.5-1.2 m. Every one of them reads to the tour as a wall at an altitude
       the drone is never at, so the tour believes regions are disconnected that
       the aircraft crosses without noticing, runs out of world, and declares the
       building finished. Measured on 2026-08-15: two consecutive runs ended
       PARTIAL_FINISH at 479.4 and 587.5 m3, the second having never gone south
       of y = -20.5 in a building that runs to -34.5: a quarter of it never
       visited, with FALCON's own frontier set empty.

       1.25 is the middle of the usable band (1.0-1.5) and the altitude the
       follower already calls mid-band and returns to when it unsticks. It is
       one value for both worlds, like every other tuning here: it sits inside
       the warehouse box (0.6-2.4) as well, and is strictly closer to where that
       aircraft flies than 1.0 was.

       It MUST stay inside the flight box. A slice outside it selects no voxels
       and the tour's model of the world goes blank rather than merely wrong,
       the same failure recorded above against raising box_min_z past 1.0. -->
  <!-- ── How far a trajectory may commit through UNOBSERVED space ──────────
       checkTrajCollision walks the executing curve six metres ahead and
       rejects a sample only if it is literally OCCUPIED. UNKNOWN passes, so
       the aircraft will fly the whole six metres into a volume it has never
       seen, on the assumption that it is empty. The follower cannot catch it
       either: its voxel brake gate accumulates OCCUPIED only, so "known to be
       empty" and "never looked at" are the same thing to it as well. Several
       of these worlds' props are hollow shells whose interiors are never
       observed and stay UNKNOWN for the whole mission, and they read to the
       planner as free space it may route through.

       Rejecting UNKNOWN outright would deadlock exploration, because
       approaching unobserved space is the entire job. What is wrong is
       COMMITTING to it. Within this radius the sensor is about to resolve the
       volume anyway (the depth camera's near clip is 0.95 m) and the aircraft
       can stop inside its own braking distance, 0.18 m at the 0.35 m/s
       ceiling; beyond it the curve is a guess.

       1.5 m rather than something tighter because the near clip guarantees a
       band of unobserved space directly ahead at all times, and a limit inside
       that band would refuse every trajectory. Unset means 1e9, i.e. upstream
       behaviour. -->
  <!-- 3.0, from 1.5. This knob has a cost on the other side and it took a
       while to see it. Refusing to commit into unobserved space is also how the
       aircraft stops APPROACHING it, and approaching unobserved space is how a
       frontier gets revealed: a viewpoint candidate must itself be in
       known-free space, so a cluster whose whole sampling ring is unobserved
       can never be given one, is retired as unviewable, and the frontier set
       empties with the building unmapped.

       Measured. Before this limit existed the hospital finished at 817 and
       822 m3. With it at 1.5 the peak rose to 856.9 m3  -  the best figure ever
       recorded here, because the aircraft stops driving into things it cannot
       see  -  but PARTIAL_FINISH legs appeared at 277-300 m3, and on one of them
       the finish amnesty re-offered 52 retired clusters, with isNearUnknown and
       the visibility bar both relaxed, and every one of them went straight back
       to dormant. Nothing was wrong with the margin; there was simply no
       observed free space to stand in.

       3.0 m still refuses the six-metre guess this was written for  -  the
       measured refusals ran to a 4.93 m median and 6.0 m max  -  while leaving
       the aircraft room to close on a frontier and reveal it. -->
  <arg name="unknown_commit_m" default="3.0"/>
  <param name="/fsm/unknown_commit_m" value="$(arg unknown_commit_m)"/>

  <arg name="ccl_slice_height" default="1.25"/>
  <param name="/map_config/ccl_slice_height" value="$(arg ccl_slice_height)"/>

  <!-- ── A* knows the aircraft has a WIDTH ─────────────────────────────────
       Upstream A* tests one voxel per node, so a 0.14 m slot between two
       clutter piles is a valid node and therefore a valid route, for an
       airframe 0.52 m across. The B-spline optimiser is no help: safe_distance
       is a SOFT cost it trades away when no curve can satisfy it, which is
       exactly the case inside a gap narrower than the margin asked for.
       Measured in the warehouse clutter cluster, where 0.14 / 0.36 / 0.38 m
       gaps meet: one run flew the 0.36 m slot at the piles' own top height,
       ground there for 45 s over 20 contact episodes, and CAPSIZED.

       0.20 m is a two-sided trade and both sides are measured. It must exceed
       half the widest gap to be refused (0.38 / 2 = 0.19) and stay under half
       the narrowest opening that must stay flyable, the hospital's 26 doorways
       at 0.930 m which this stack threads today:

         0.14 / 0.36 / 0.38 m clutter slots  ->  closed
         0.930 m hospital doorway            ->  0.53 m still open
         0.909 m narrowest warehouse aisle   ->  0.51 m still open

       start_clearance suppresses the skirt near the search start, because an
       aircraft that has drifted against a wall is inside its own inflation and
       could otherwise never plan its way off it. 0 disables both and restores
       upstream point-robot search. -->
  <!-- ── Places the aircraft has PROVED it cannot get through ──────────────
       The dead-end guard retires the FRONTIER at such a place, which stops the
       coverage tour targeting it. Nothing stopped A* ROUTING through it, so the
       aircraft was sent back in on the way to somewhere else and caught again.
       Measured in two consecutive hospital legs, both ending in a CAPSIZE at
       the same coordinates: 834 s spent within two metres of (-2.3, -18.0)
       among a scrubs cart and an instrument cart, 22 bumper reports, 13
       viewpoints blocked and 11 frontiers retired around it, and it was still
       routed back in until it flipped. Every guard fired correctly and none
       could stop the aircraft going there.

       A* now reads the frontier finder's own persisted blocked regions and
       treats each as a disc to route around. It is a PREFERENCE: tested on the
       strict pass only, so if avoiding it means no route at all, search()
       retries without it and the worst case is the route the planner would
       have taken anyway. Regions also expire on their existing TTL ladder.

       1.0 m: wide enough to push a route out of the trap that caught the
       aircraft, narrow enough that it does not straddle a 2 m hospital
       corridor on its own. 0 disables. -->
  <arg name="astar_no_go_radius"    default="1.0"/>
  <arg name="astar_no_go_refresh_s" default="2.0"/>
  <param name="/astar/no_go_radius"    value="$(arg astar_no_go_radius)"/>
  <param name="/astar/no_go_refresh_s" value="$(arg astar_no_go_refresh_s)"/>

  <!-- 0.26, the airframe's true half-width (collision mesh 0.52 m across the
       arms), NOT a compromise value. With a lattice fine enough to resolve what
       is left (see astar_resolution below), "A* found a route" then means
       exactly "the aircraft fits", which is the only version of this test that
       is right in both worlds.

       0.20 was a compromise between closing the warehouse's 0.14-0.38 m clutter
       slots and not closing the hospital's 0.930 m doorways, and it needed a
       fallback to a point-robot route when no inflated route existed. That
       fallback then handed the aircraft straight back into the clutter slots:
       61 fallbacks in one warehouse leg, 278 bumper reports across 32 episodes
       on four clutter piles, and a CAPSIZE at 103.9 m3  -  against CLEAN at
       352.1 m3 with zero contacts on the same world before it. The doorway
       problem was never that the aircraft did not fit; it was that the SEARCH
       LATTICE could not represent the gap. Fix the lattice, not the width. -->
  <arg name="astar_inflate_radius"  default="0.20"/>
  <arg name="astar_start_clearance" default="0.50"/>
  <param name="/astar/inflate_radius"  value="$(arg astar_inflate_radius)"/>
  <param name="/astar/start_clearance" value="$(arg astar_start_clearance)"/>

  <!-- 0.3, from 0.5, and this is what makes the inflation above honest. A
       0.930 m hospital doorway inflated by the 0.26 m half-width leaves a
       0.41 m band of legal centre positions; on a 0.5 m lattice no node need
       land inside it, so a doorway the aircraft fits through reads as sealed.
       Measured at 0.20 inflation / 0.5 lattice: 1068 "No path to next
       viewpoint" lines in one hospital run, against 0 in a healthy one.

       0.3 was tried before and abandoned, but for a reason that no longer
       applies: it needs 248,889 nodes over this map against upstream's 100,000
       pool, and every query exhausted it. astar_allocate_num is 500000 now,
       sized for exactly this. max_search_time goes up with it, because a finer
       lattice is more nodes to expand. -->
  <arg name="astar_resolution"      default="0.5"/>
  <arg name="astar_max_search_time" default="0.05"/>
  <arg name="astar_allocate_num"    default="500000"/>
  <param name="/astar/profile/default/resolution"      value="$(arg astar_resolution)"/>
  <param name="/astar/profile/default/max_search_time" value="$(arg astar_max_search_time)"/>
  <param name="/astar/allocate_num"                    value="$(arg astar_allocate_num)"/>

  <!-- ── How far FALCON may plan from a pose the aircraft is not at ───────
       falcon_replan_from_pose.patch corrects the replan origin to odometry
       once it has drifted past this. The origin drifts because FALCON derives
       it by evaluating the PREVIOUS trajectory at the current instant rather
       than by reading odometry, and t_r saturates at the curve's duration, so
       every second this stack spends braked, held or retreating (which is most
       of the seconds it spends near an obstacle) opens a gap.

       1.5 was chosen to end a runaway where the gap reached 5.4 m, and it does.
       But it also LICENSES a gap of up to 1.5 m as normal, and that is not a
       constant cost across worlds: in a 1.4 m warehouse aisle the aircraft has
       0.70 m of half width and a metre of origin error is absorbed by the
       optimiser; in a 0.90 m hospital doorway it has 0.45 m, so a plan anchored
       1.5 m away is a plan whose first metre lies in the wall. Measured: 82
       corrections in one hospital run, every one of them 1.5 to 2.1 m, i.e. the
       threshold sat permanently saturated.

       0.40 is the follower's own acquisition tolerance. Under it the previous
       curve's endpoint is the BETTER origin (it joins the trajectory being
       flown smoothly, with no velocity step); over it the curve is a fiction
       and odometry wins. -->
  <arg name="replan_from_pose_drift" default="0.40"/>
  <param name="/fsm/replan_from_pose_drift" value="$(arg replan_from_pose_drift)"/>

  <!-- ── When an unpublishable destination is given up on ─────────────────
       PUB_TRAJ rejects a plan when either the optimised curve or the initial
       path samples one occupied voxel inside its first six metres, and
       checkTrajCollision is a point test with no radius while safe_distance is
       a soft cost the optimiser may trade away. A curve fitted through a tight
       doorway therefore cuts the corner its own waypoints went round, fails the
       check, and nothing is published. The FSM replans, the tour offers the same
       viewpoint, and the identical pair of curves comes back: measured 1781 of
       1808 publish attempts in one hospital run, 823 in another, with the
       aircraft holding station because an unpublished plan is indistinguishable
       from a dead planner from outside the node.

       After this many consecutive failures against the same destination, hand
       it to the frontier finder's blacklist, which expires and escalates on its
       own. 4 is deliberately small: the evidence is direct and already proved,
       unlike the dead-end guard's 25 s of confinement, which is a slow and
       indirect way to notice the same thing.

       The streak must span real TIME, and that is not a detail. PUB_TRAJ is
       entered on every FSM tick, so four consecutive failures is a third of a
       second and a sustained deadlock becomes one shadow every four ticks:
       measured 192 of them in a 481 s run, each covering a 2 m radius, which
       sterilised the map faster than the aircraft could explore it and left the
       mission confined to one corner of the building at 146.0 m3. A count alone
       was never the right test. -->
  <arg name="publish_fail_blacklist_after" default="4"/>
  <arg name="publish_fail_same_target_m"   default="1.0"/>
  <arg name="publish_fail_min_span_s"      default="6.0"/>
  <arg name="publish_fail_cooldown_s"      default="20.0"/>
  <param name="/fsm/publish_fail_blacklist_after"
         value="$(arg publish_fail_blacklist_after)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/publish_fail_same_target_m"
         value="$(arg publish_fail_same_target_m)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/publish_fail_min_span_s"
         value="$(arg publish_fail_min_span_s)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/publish_fail_cooldown_s"
         value="$(arg publish_fail_cooldown_s)"/>

  <!-- ── Which speed a "slow" trajectory is re-timed onto ─────────────────
       On seeing a trajectory averaging under trigger_vel, FALCON rewrites its
       knot vector so it would average target_vel instead. Upstream's target is
       2.0 m/s, which on this aircraft is not a correction but a compression:
       measured 324 rescales in one 16-minute hospital mission, ratios clustered
       at 0.08 to 0.25, i.e. every seventh plan handed the follower a reference
       four to thirteen times faster than the 0.15 m/s the optimiser had just
       been told was the limit. The follower cannot legally chase it, so the gap
       it opens is the same gap replan_from_pose_drift above keeps repairing.

       Two things are set here. The target becomes the configured cruise, so a
       trajectory already flying at the speed it was asked for is left alone.
       And ratio_min is 1.0, which permits the rescale to STRETCH time only.
       That direction is a genuine safety valve - a trajectory whose time
       allocation is too tight gets slowed to something flyable - while the
       compressing direction is exactly what breaks a velocity feasibility the
       B-spline optimiser had already guaranteed. Nothing here can now speed a
       reference up.

       This also removes the divide-by-zero behind the node's aborts: a
       trajectory that does not turn has avg_yaw_vel 0, and 1.57/0 fed through
       std::min produced a non-finite re-timing whose knot vector later killed
       exploration_node from inside its own visualiser. -->
  <arg name="slow_traj_target_vel"  default="0.15"/>
  <arg name="slow_traj_target_yaw"  default="1.57"/>
  <arg name="slow_traj_trigger_vel" default="0.5"/>
  <arg name="slow_traj_ratio_min"   default="1.0"/>
  <arg name="slow_traj_ratio_max"   default="2.0"/>
  <param name="/fsm/slow_traj_target_vel"  value="$(arg slow_traj_target_vel)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/slow_traj_target_yaw"  value="$(arg slow_traj_target_yaw)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/slow_traj_trigger_vel" value="$(arg slow_traj_trigger_vel)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/slow_traj_ratio_min"   value="$(arg slow_traj_ratio_min)"/>
  <param name="/fsm/slow_traj_ratio_max"   value="$(arg slow_traj_ratio_max)"/>

  <!-- traj_server parks its pre-plan command at the init pose, no ramp:
       the follower owns takeoff. -->
  <param name="/traj_server/init_dx" value="0.0"/>
  <param name="/traj_server/init_dy" value="0.0"/>
  <param name="/traj_server/init_dz" value="0.0"/>

  <!-- ── The adapter nodes ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <node pkg="falcon_adapter" type="sensor_pose_node.py" name="sensor_pose_node"
        output="screen">
    <param name="odom_topic" value="$(arg drone_ns)/odom"/>
  </node>

  <!-- ── FALCON, unmodified ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <!-- respawn rather than required: FALCON's in-process LKH TSP solver
       segfaults intermittently (FindTour -> Best5OptMove -> Flip_SL, an upstream
       heuristic bug), and a `required` node takes the whole mission down with
       it. Respawning restarts the planner in place; the map is rebuilt from the
       depth stream that keeps flowing. A tight crash loop is still visible in
       the log (respawn_delay spaces them), which is the signal to stop. -->
  <node pkg="exploration_manager" name="exploration_node" type="exploration_node"
        output="screen" respawn="true" respawn_delay="3">
    <!-- /odom_world comes from sensor_pose_node (world-frame twist), NOT the
         raw bridged odometry, whose twist is body-frame per REP-147 and would
         start every replan with a velocity pointing along the nose. -->
    <remap from="/voxel_mapping/depth_image"     to="$(arg drone_ns)/front_depth/depth/image_raw"/>
    <remap from="/transformer/sensor_pose_topic" to="/map_ros/pose"/>
  </node>

  <node pkg="fast_planner" name="traj_server" type="traj_server" output="screen"/>

  <!-- Drone marker for RViz. FALCON's rviz.rviz displays the robot from
       /odom_visualization/robot, but the node that publishes it belongs to
       FALCON's own uav_simulator bringup, which this stack replaces with the
       real Gazebo aircraft; without this, RViz shows the map growing with no
       drone in it. The node reads ~odometry, remapped to the world-frame odom. -->
  <node pkg="odom_visualization" name="odom_visualization" type="odom_visualization">
    <remap from="~odometry" to="/odom_world"/>
    <param name="robot_scale" value="2.0"/>
    <param name="color/r" value="1.0"/>
    <param name="color/g" value="0.3"/>
    <param name="color/b" value="0.0"/>
  </node>

  <!-- ── Mission watchdog: end a run that has stopped earning its clock ──
       An exploration fails in ways every node here reads as success. The FSM
       is in EXEC_TRAJ, the follower is tracking, the aircraft is moving, and
       the map has not gained a voxel in four minutes because the drone is
       orbiting a room whose only exit is a doorway it cannot thread. The two
       facts that settle it (where the aircraft has been, and how much map that
       bought) are not in the same place anywhere else in this graph.

       The judgement is in core/planning/exploration/progress_monitor.py and is
       unit tested against both the stuck shapes and the healthy missions they
       are confused with: a slow thorough sweep of one crowded room is confined
       and must NOT be aborted; a long transit through mapped corridors is
       barren and must not either. It takes both at once.

       It publishes /mission/nudge (recoverable: the follower answers with a
       fresh survey turn) and /mission/abort, and prints a greppable banner the
       campaign harness ends the run on. It never kills anything itself, so an
       interactive session is not torn down under its operator. -->
  <arg name="watchdog"             default="true"/>
  <arg name="watchdog_time_cap_s"  default="2400.0"/>
  <!-- Radius and growth are a PAIR and only fire together. 3.0 m is above the
       largest legitimate stationary maneuver (a survey turn is a point, a
       retreat 1.3 m) and below the smallest useful exploration leg. 3.0 m3/min
       is a third of the rate a healthy hospital run sustains (measured: 430 m3
       in 11 min = 39 m3/min) and well above mapper dither. -->
  <arg name="watchdog_confine_radius_m"  default="3.0"/>
  <arg name="watchdog_growth_m3_per_min" default="3.0"/>
  <arg name="watchdog_confine_cap_s"     default="240.0"/>
  <arg name="watchdog_barren_cap_s"      default="300.0"/>
  <arg name="watchdog_grace_s"           default="90.0"/>
  <!-- 180, from the node's own 120. The follower now deliberately HOLDS for up
       to 90 s when a place has defeated it three times, so the aircraft can be
       legitimately stationary far longer than it used to be and the watchdog
       must not read that as pinned. It still ends a run that stays put through
       two of the longest holds the follower is able to take. -->
  <arg name="watchdog_no_move_cap_s"     default="180.0"/>
  <node if="$(arg watchdog)" pkg="falcon_adapter" type="mission_watchdog_node.py"
        name="mission_watchdog" output="screen">
    <param name="odom_topic"         value="$(arg drone_ns)/odom"/>
    <param name="time_cap_s"         value="$(arg watchdog_time_cap_s)"/>
    <param name="grace_s"            value="$(arg watchdog_grace_s)"/>
    <param name="confine_radius_m"   value="$(arg watchdog_confine_radius_m)"/>
    <param name="growth_m3_per_min"  value="$(arg watchdog_growth_m3_per_min)"/>
    <param name="confine_cap_s"      value="$(arg watchdog_confine_cap_s)"/>
    <param name="barren_cap_s"       value="$(arg watchdog_barren_cap_s)"/>
    <param name="no_move_cap_s"      value="$(arg watchdog_no_move_cap_s)"/>
  </node>

  <!-- ── Our follower: takeoff, survey turn, then fly the B-spline ─────── -->
  <!-- Tracking authority, forwarded so a run can be tuned without editing a
       file. Defaults match bspline_follower.launch exactly, so forwarding
       them changes nothing by itself.

       max_overspeed is a CLEARANCE budget, not a comfort setting: FALCON
       checks its curve against the map at the speed it planned, and flying the
       same curve faster spends that margin on stopping distance. It is only
       safe to raise it in step with safe_distance — which is why these two
       knobs are named together here. max_overspeed >= max_catchup_speed is
       enforced by VelocityServoParams and raises if violated. -->
  <!-- ── The speed ceiling is DERIVED from the clearance, not chosen ──────
       The follower must never be allowed to fly at a speed whose stopping
       distance exceeds the margin the planner reserved. That margin is
           safe_distance (0.45) - airframe half width (0.26) = 0.19 m
       and the stopping distance at speed v, with this airframe's measured
       0.30 s of command latency and 0.8 m/s^2 of braking, is
           d(v) = 0.30 v + v^2 / 1.6
       d(0.25) = 0.11 m, inside the margin. d(0.35) = 0.18 m, AT it.
       d(0.40) = 0.22 m, over it. d(0.60) = 0.41 m, more than twice it.

       The servo's per-tick ceiling is exactly `planned_speed + max_overspeed`
       (servo.py:320 passes it as max_speed_xy to limit_velocity, overriding the
       limits' own), so max_overspeed is not a cruise setting. It is the
       CORRECTION BUDGET: everything the position loop has above the
       feedforward, which is what closes a cross-track error.

       That distinction cost a run. Cutting max_overspeed from 0.35 to 0.10 to
       bound the absolute speed also cut the authority to correct, and
       cross-track errors that used to close in a second began to persist,
       straight into the clearance-breach retreat. Measured: the aircraft
       stalled at a 0.93 m doorway it had flown through in the previous
       configuration, retreating on breaches of 0.27 to 0.51 m with 0.32 to
       0.54 m of cross-track, coverage frozen at 670 m3.

       The right lever is the PLAN, not the correction. max_vel 0.20 with
       max_overspeed 0.15 puts the ceiling on the same 0.35 m/s while leaving
       0.15 m/s of correction everywhere, and because the ceiling is relative,
       leaving it in the slow sections too, which is where a corner and a
       doorway both are.

       The old 0.6 / 0.35 / 0.25 let the servo sprint to 0.60 m/s to close a
       lag against a 0.25 m/s plan, i.e. fly at 2.4x the speed FALCON checked
       its curve at, spending the whole clearance budget on stopping distance
       before anything had gone wrong. Measured in the first acceptance
       attempt: every contact was at 0.48-0.59 m/s, all of them the aircraft
       accelerating into an obstacle the map did not yet have.

       The cost is wall-clock and it is small: the aircraft spends most of a
       mission at plan speed anyway, and the catch-up being given up was only
       ever used after a hold or a retreat. Raise these ONLY together with
       safe_distance. -->
  <arg name="max_speed_xy"      default="0.35"/>
  <arg name="max_overspeed"     default="0.20"/>
  <arg name="max_catchup_speed" default="0.15"/>

  <include file="$(find falcon_adapter)/launch/bspline_follower.launch">
    <arg name="drone_ns"          value="$(arg drone_ns)"/>
    <arg name="unknown_brake"     value="$(arg unknown_brake)"/>
    <arg name="max_speed_xy"      value="$(arg max_speed_xy)"/>
    <arg name="max_overspeed"     value="$(arg max_overspeed)"/>
    <arg name="max_catchup_speed" value="$(arg max_catchup_speed)"/>
  </include>
</launch>
